<article class="env-corollary" id="066Y">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/066Y">Corollary <span data-tag="066Y">9.3.5.25</span>.</a> Let $\kappa $ and $\lambda $ be regular cardinals, where $\lambda $ is uncountable, and let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \lambda }$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories. Each of the following conditions implies the next: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ is $\kappa $-left exact. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ is $\kappa $-flat. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ preserves all $\kappa $-small limits which exist in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $\kappa $-complete, then all three conditions are equivalent. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      The implication $(1) \Rightarrow (2)$ follows from Remark <a data-tag="066R" href="/tag/066R">9.3.5.16</a> and the implication $(2) \Rightarrow (3)$ is Corollary <a data-tag="0662" href="/tag/0662">9.3.4.23</a>. If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $\kappa $-complete, then the implication $(3) \Rightarrow (1)$ is a special case of Theorem <a data-tag="066X" href="/tag/066X">9.3.5.23</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
